WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.120
American science journalist,
Joshua Foer,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.880
always thought his brain
was pretty average.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.800
But one day in 2005,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.280
while covering the
USA National Memory Championship,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.680
a competition designed to test
the limits of the human brain,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.800
he's presented with an
unusual opportunity -

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.800
a former champion offers to
train him to become a mental athlete.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.520
Over the course of the year,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.000
Joshua learns ancient methods
like memory palaces.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.800
This is where you associate
pieces of information

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.400
with a location in your mind
to make them unforgettable.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.920
He also uses cutting-edge
mnemonic techniques,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.840
which allow him to remember hundreds
of playing cards in a specific order.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.280
Soon, Joshua's competitive instinct
takes over.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.440
He trains in his parents' basement
for hours a day.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.000
By 2006, he's ready to attend
the championship again,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.000
but this time not as a journalist
but as a competitor.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.160
Round after round,
Joshua the newcomer

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.880
gives former champions
a run for their money.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.040
He makes it to the final.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.160
Despite being up against
a formidable opponent,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.120
he uses the brain training methods
he was taught...

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.600
...and wins.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.320
How could a novice like Joshua
become a champion so quickly?

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.640
Does this mean that anybody
could become a mental athlete?

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.000
To really understand
Joshua's success,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.240
we need to consider
the role of opportunity.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.040
It was long thought

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.920
that richer children did better
than poorer children in school

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.600
because they were more
'naturally gifted'.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.560
But now we know that it is times
when children aren't in school,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.880
including the long school holidays,
when poorer children lack access

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.600
to extracurricular activities,
that often creates an attainment gap.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.560
Nutrition and access to technology
also make a contribution,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.920
which is partly why IQ scores
across the globe

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.640
rose an average of 30 points
in the 20th Century,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.640
factors that have literally nothing
to do with genetics.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.840
When it comes to differences
in outcome,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.080
it's not nature but nurture
which has the greater impact,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.000
and it's the same with Joshua Foer.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.560
His expert training,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.160
access to special techniques
his opponents hadn't yet discovered,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.760
and a quiet place to study -
his parents' basement -

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.760
that all contributed to his success.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.600
Though it's also worth knowing that
despite being a memory champion,

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.760
even Joshua can sometimes forget
where he put his car keys.

